# Volleyball Athlete Forecasting

Athlete-specific, next-match stat forecasting with TensorFlow. The repository
connects to Volleyball World's public competition and match routes, collects
match-level athlete history from an authorized JSON endpoint or the public player
page fallback, then predicts:

- total points
- attack points
- block points
- serve points

The model is deliberately evaluated against simple last-value and rolling-mean
baselines using chronological splits. A neural network is not treated as useful
unless it beats those baselines on held-out future matches.

> Independent research project. Not affiliated with Volleyball World or FIVB.
> Review Volleyball World's current terms and obtain permission before commercial
> use, high-volume collection, or redistribution of source data.

## Architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  VW[Volleyball World] -->|competitions / schedules / tournament API| C[Cached API client]
  VW -->|authorized athlete JSON or public player page| I[Ingestion adapter]
  C --> N[Canonical match table]
  I --> N
  N --> V[Validation and DNP policy]
  V --> F[Leakage-safe features]
  F --> B[Persistence and rolling baselines]
  F --> T[TensorFlow LSTM + next-match context]
  B --> E[Chronological evaluation]
  T --> E
  E --> A[Saved model, metrics, model card]
  A --> P[CLI or FastAPI forecast]
```

## Observed source routes

The client includes these configurable routes under
`https://en.volleyballworld.com`:

```text
GET /api/v1/globalschedule/competitions/{year}/{month}
GET /api/v1/globalschedule/{start_date}/{end_date}
GET /api/v1/volley-tournament/{start_date}/{end_date}/{tournament_no}
GET /volleyball/competitions/{competition_slug}/{season}/players/{player_id}
```

The first three return competition or match context. The last route is the
server-rendered player profile used only when no authorized athlete JSON endpoint
has been configured. These interfaces are undocumented and can change; all access
is isolated behind `VolleyballWorldClient` and parser tests.

## Quick start

Python 3.11 is recommended.

```bash
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
python -m pip install -e ".[ml,api,dev]"
cp .env.example .env
```

Inspect competitions:

```bash
volley-forecast discover-competitions --year 2025 --month 6
```

Create `config/athlete.local.yaml` from the example, then ingest multiple seasons:

```bash
volley-forecast ingest-player \
  --config config/athlete.local.yaml \
  --output data/processed/athlete_matches.csv \
  --raw-dir data/raw
```

Analyze athlete form before modelling:

```bash
volley-forecast analyze \
  --data data/processed/athlete_matches.csv \
  --recent-window 5
```

Train and evaluate:

```bash
volley-forecast train \
  --data data/processed/athlete_matches.csv \
  --model-dir artifacts/athlete \
  --config config/model.example.yaml
```

Forecast the next match:

```bash
volley-forecast forecast \
  --model-dir artifacts/athlete \
  --history data/processed/athlete_matches.csv \
  --next-date 2026-08-20 \
  --opponent Italy
```

Serve the same model over HTTP:

```bash
volley-forecast serve --model-dir artifacts/athlete --port 8000
```

## Run without live data

The committed CSV is synthetic and exists only to exercise the pipeline:

```bash
volley-forecast train \
  --data data/sample/demo_athlete_matches.csv \
  --model-dir artifacts/demo \
  --config config/model.example.yaml
```

## Why this design

- **Athlete-specific:** each model learns one athlete's form, workload, rest, and
  opponent-history effects rather than averaging unlike roles.
- **No random split:** validation and test samples occur after training samples.
- **Known next-match context:** rest days, season timing, and prior performance
  against the named opponent enter a separate context branch.
- **Small-data controls:** early stopping, compact networks, baselines, minimum
  sample checks, and explicit promotion status.
- **Uncertainty:** Monte Carlo dropout produces configurable lower and upper bands.
- **Replaceable ingestion:** site changes are limited to client or parser adapters.

## Repository layout

```text
src/volley_forecast/   client, parser, analysis, features, model, training, inference, API
config/                athlete, JSON-mapping, and model examples
data/sample/           synthetic demonstration data only
docs/                  architecture, contract, model card, API notes, roadmap
tests/                 offline fixtures and unit tests
.github/workflows/     core CI and opt-in TensorFlow smoke test
```

## Minimum data guidance

The code can run with 20 participating matches, but that is a technical floor,
not a quality guarantee. Prefer several seasons and at least 40-60 appearances.
Keep role, competition level, and scoring rules reasonably consistent. Model
quality should be judged by held-out MAE per target and by lift over baselines.

